    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with a potent offensive outlook, led by our speed-first strategy. **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (77.01) sits atop our efficiency rankings; his leadoff role against Teng provides the perfect environment for his elite sprint speed to manifest in stolen base production, bolstered by a significant 14% dynamic platoon edge. He is closely followed by **Yordan Alvarez** (76.23) and **Christian Yelich** (75.84). **Yordan Alvarez** remains our most reliable high-floor anchor, leveraging an elite 0.419 xwOBA against Rea, while **Christian Yelich** capitalizes on the soft matchup against Sasaki (5.11 xERA) with a massive 13% platoon advantage that keeps him in the elite tier despite being slotted in the four-hole. **Trea Turner** (73.30) rounds out our top-tier plays, where his favorable 10% leadoff boost and the \"slow delivery\" profile of Cecconi suggest a high probability for a multi-steal day.\n\nThe optimizer faced a difficult exclusion decision regarding our bench, as the roster is currently thinned by the unfortunate rainout of **Junior Caminero**. While **Caleb Durbin** hovered near the threshold, his absence from the confirmed starting lineup resulted in an automatic benching. We are keeping **Kazuma Okamoto** on the pine today; despite his talent, he draws a brutal matchup against Skenes (2.96 xERA), which effectively cratered his score projection. We are proceeding with **Gary Sanchez** and **Luke Raley** in the five-hole spots; while their individual scores are mid-range, the projected favorable SP matchups against Sasaki and Kolek provide just enough leverage to maintain their positions over reserve options like **Colt Keith**, **Zac Veen**, **Lars Nootbaar**, **Dillon Dingler**, **Josh Smith**, **Luis Pena**, or **Pedro Ramirez**. Owners should monitor the weather closely, as any further precipitation in the late window could force a scramble for late-inning replacements, but for now, the path to maximizing our stolen base and batting average categories remains clear.",
